Ray Dillinger wrote:
> Early versions of Nethack had "multiple keys" or "random keys"
> on the theory that "different keys open different doors" and
> in order to open a door with a key, you had to go through your
> collection of keys trying each in turn until you found the "right"
> one.  I think there were a total of 8 different keys.

I recently posted a YANI involving a return to multiple key types that
solved the issues you presented (basically keys would be a random type,
with different kinds having different purposes, and there would only be
a small number of types).  Didn't stop Certain People from complaining,
loudly and bitterly, about the very idea.

You'd have thought I had been talking about abortion, or had favored
Mike over Joel, or something.

However, they were correct in the area that having multiple, generic
key types and random locks probably doesn't make the game more
interesting.  The penalty for kicking down doors in the current system
is simply not great enough to bother with all those keys unless there
is some greater difference between them than the generic random doors
they unlock.

Further, if you increased the penalty for kicking down a door, or made
it radically harder to do so, issues of game navigation come up.  What
if the stairs is behind a locked door the player doesn't have?  Such
problems as that are potentially solvable, but it would be a real chore
to do so, and then there's always the matter of keeping the game
winnable if a nymph steals an important key, or a key gets destroyed,
or such things.

